            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house is extremely leaky, with over 14 air changes per hour measured during my testing.



I recommend that you seal the area under your bathroom cabinet where the pipes come in to the house.



I recommend that you complete the air sealing around your new front door, and replace weatherstripping on both front and back doors if necessary. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

 

Air leakage was also observed through both of the bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit. Using bathroom fans will help control moisture within your home, which will become more important as you tighten the building envelope.



While you can do targetted air sealing work (and I recommend that you do some of this, especially under your bathroom cabinet), other upgrades discussed in this report may also affect your air leakage rate. 



Savings: For a minimum 27% improvement you could potentially save $560 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, and your back porch appears to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $830 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 24.8 GJ, and qualify for up to$2500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for an insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was assumed to have 6 inches (R20) of blown fiberglass insulation. As you mentioned you may be interested in adding more insulation, I have modelled adding 9 inches (R30) of blown cellulose insulation for a total insulation value of R50.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation in your attic to R50, you could potentially save $490 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $375 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for ceiling insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a ceiling insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</ceilings>
                <windows>

As most of your windows are older double pane windows, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all the older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



While most of your windows are older double pane windows, they do appear to have a low-E coating and are for the most part in fair physical condition. 



The large front window next to your wood stove is already a triple paned window, so I have not upgraded that in my model. 



I also noted that the back bathroom window that opens into the cold storage is a single paned window with an aluminium frame. I recommend making replacing this window a priority. 



Savings: 

By replacing the 7 dining room windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $150 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.5 GJ.  Windows that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified and rated for climate zone 3 are eligible for $250 per window in rebates, to a max of 12 windows through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 80% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, which also incorporates an ECM fan blower motor. If you choose a high-efficiency propane furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion propane-fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, that also incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $870 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 29.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for the furnace will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a furnace rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



WOOD STOVE

As you are planning on upgrading your wood stove, I have modelled the potential savings. AEA recommends a ULC Certified wood stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air duct.



Savings:

By a Certified wood stove and using it for 25% of your heating load, you could potentially save $1,000 per year in energy savings, and qualify for up to 1/2 of pre-tax purchase cost of the stove to a maximum of $1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program





FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh

Oil  - $1.27 per litre 

Propane - $0.865 per litre

Wood - $315.00 per cord 

Pellets - $7.35 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

